  "Q1": {
    "answer": "Peskas is connected to a variety of entities, including being referenced by the paper \"How Much Is Too Much Information? Digital Feedback on Fisher Behavior (Kenya)\" and the Dataverse Project. It references concepts such as Fish & Aquatic-Food Nutrition, Zanzibar, WorldFish, and the Harvard Dataverse. Peskas also has several components, including the Peskas Automated Validation Engine, Peskas Tracks App, Peskas COASTS (regional analysis platform), Peskas Data Ingestion & Analytics Pipeline, peskas.timor.portal (R package), peskas.timor.pipeline (R package), Peskas Web Dashboard (Shiny), and Peskas History & Global Scaling Timeline. It is conceptually related to PeskAAS (Peskas Automated Analytics System) and the COASTS (Small Scale Fisheries Analysis Platform). Peskas is also associated with datasets like Mozambique Validated Trips, Zanzibar Validated Trips, and Kenya Validated Trips. It is referenced by FishBase, KoboToolbox, and the WIOMSA Data Harmonization Presentation (Oct 2025). Peskas is also connected to individuals like Alexander Tilley, who produced it, and is referenced by the Nairobi Convention, and countries such as Tanzania, Malawi, Timor-Leste, Mozambique, and Kenya. It is also related to the concept of Small-Scale Fisheries (SSF), the Asia-Africa BlueTech Superhighway (AABS), Fishery Nutrient Profile (FNP), ProFishBlue, and IUU Fishing Monitoring. The paper \"Fishery Nutrient Profiles for Nutrition-Sensitive SSF Management in Timor-Leste (Nature Food 2026)\" shares data with Peskas, and Beach Management Units (BMUs) also share data with Peskas. Additionally, Peskas is conceptually related to Functional Requirements for Electronic Data Collection Tools.",
